### Why do dynamic IDs cause flakiness?

Dynamic IDs and attributes come from frameworks that generate markup at build or render time. A selector pinned to a generated class matches today and misses tomorrow, producing failures that look like bugs but are really locator rot.

```typescript
// FRAGILE: tied to generated IDs that change every build
await page.locator('#btn-9f3a2').click();           // Breaks next build
await page.locator('.css-1a2b3c > div > button').click(); // Breaks on restructure
await page.locator('//div[@class="generated"]/button').click(); // XPath, same problem
```

### How does getByRole work?

getByRole finds an element by its accessibility role and accessible name, which mirrors how users and screen readers identify controls. It is the most recommended locator.

```typescript
// STABLE: role-based locators
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign In' }).click();
await page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Dashboard' }).isVisible();
await page.getByRole('link', { name: 'View Statement' }).click();
await page.getByRole('textbox', { name: 'Amount' }).fill('5000');
await page.getByRole('checkbox', { name: 'Remember me' }).check();
await page.getByRole('alert').textContent(); // Error messages
```

### How does getByLabel work?

getByLabel finds form fields by their associated label text. Users identify inputs by their labels, not their internal IDs.

```typescript
// Form fields by label
await page.getByLabel('Username').fill('rajesh@nationalbank.in');
await page.getByLabel('Password').fill(process.env.TEST_PASS!);
await page.getByLabel('Transfer Amount').fill('50000');
await page.getByLabel('Beneficiary Account').fill('ACC-002');
```

### When should you use getByText?

getByText is appropriate when the visible text is genuinely the right identifier, such as a confirmation message or a uniquely labelled link.

```typescript
// Content assertions by visible text
await expect(page.getByText('Transfer Successful')).toBeVisible();
await expect(page.getByText('Insufficient Balance')).toBeVisible();
await page.getByText('View all transactions').click();
```

### When should you use getByTestId?

When no user-facing handle exists, add a deliberate stable test attribute. This is a contract between developers and testers.

```html
<!-- Developer adds a stable test ID -->
<div data-testid="account-balance-widget">
  <span>Rs 9,50,000.00</span>
</div>
```

```typescript
// Test uses the stable test ID
const balance = await page.getByTestId('account-balance-widget').textContent();
```

### What is the locator preference order?

| Priority | Locator | When to Use | Stability |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `getByRole` | Interactive elements, headings, landmarks | Best |
| 2 | `getByLabel` | Form fields (inputs, selects) | Best |
| 3 | `getByText` | Content, messages, unique links | Good |
| 4 | `getByPlaceholder` | Inputs without labels (fix the label!) | Good |
| 5 | `getByTestId` | No semantic handle, decorative elements | Good |
| 6 | CSS/XPath | Last resort, always scoped | Fragile |

```typescript
// Complete Page Object using the preference order
import { Page, Locator } from '@playwright/test';

export class TransferPage {
  readonly fromAccount: Locator;     // getByLabel (form field)
  readonly toAccount: Locator;       // getByLabel (form field)
  readonly amount: Locator;          // getByLabel (form field)
  readonly transferButton: Locator;  // getByRole (button)
  readonly successMessage: Locator;  // getByText (content)
  readonly balanceWidget: Locator;   // getByTestId (no semantic handle)

  constructor(private page: Page) {
    this.fromAccount = page.getByLabel('From Account');
    this.toAccount = page.getByLabel('To Account');
    this.amount = page.getByLabel('Amount');
    this.transferButton = page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Transfer' });
    this.successMessage = page.getByText('Transfer Successful');
    this.balanceWidget = page.getByTestId('account-balance');
  }
}
```

### How do you scope locators for precision?

When a selector matches multiple elements, scope it within a container.

```typescript
// Scoped: find the Transfer button within a specific row
const accountRow = page.getByRole('row', { name: 'ACC-001' });
await accountRow.getByRole('button', { name: 'Transfer' }).click();

// Without scoping, 'Transfer' button might match in every row
```

### Why do stable locators improve accessibility?

Writing role-based tests nudges the application toward correct ARIA roles and labels. If a button does not have an accessible name, the test cannot find it, which exposes the accessibility gap.

```typescript
// This test will fail if the button lacks an accessible name
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Submit Transfer' }).click();
// Developer is forced to add aria-label or visible text
// Result: both the test and screen readers work
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What if two buttons have the same text?" Scope one within its container using `.filter()` or a parent locator.
- "Should developers add test IDs to every element?" No. Only where no semantic locator exists. Over-using test IDs bypasses accessibility alignment.
- "How do you handle localized applications?" Use getByRole with ARIA labels that are language-independent, or use getByTestId for localized text.
